/T (The wget command lets us download files from the web over HTTP, almost as if we were accessing them through a browser. All we need to do is provide the web address of the file. For example, if we want to download a file called myfile.txt, the command would look like this: wget https://assets.tryhackme.com/additional/linux-fundamentals/part3/myfile.txt This retrieves the file directly into our current directory.)
/E (The wget command lets us download files from the web over HTTP, almost as if we were accessing them through a browser. All we need to do is provide the web address of the file. For example, if we want to download a file called myfile.txt, the command would look like this: wget https://assets.tryhackme.com/additional/linux-fundamentals/part3/myfile.txt This retrieves the file directly into our current directory.)
