/T (# What is NTFS? NTFS, which stands for New Technology File System, is the primary and most modern file system used by Microsoft Windows operating systems. Think of it as the "rulebook" or the "filing manager" that controls how data is stored, organized, and retrieved on a hard drive, SSD, or USB flash drive. NTFS is known as a journaling file system. In case of a failure, the file system can automatically repair the folders/files on disk using information stored in a log file. This function is not possible with FAT.  )
/E (# What is NTFS? NTFS, which stands for New Technology File System, is the primary and most modern file system used by Microsoft Windows operating systems. Think of it as the "rulebook" or the "filing manager" that controls how data is stored, organized, and retrieved on a hard drive, SSD, or USB flash drive. NTFS is known as a journaling file system. In case of a failure, the file system can automatically repair the folders/files on disk using information stored in a log file. This function is not possible with FAT.  )
/Pg 89 0 R
/K [3 4]
>>
endobj
